#{["Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator, because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(1+\\tan^2 x) = 2\\tan{x}\\sec^2 x\n$$\n\nSo, let\n\n$$\nu = 1+\\tan^2 x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2\\tan x\\sec^2 x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u = \\tan x\\sec^2 x\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi/4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{π/4}\\frac{\\tan x\\sec^{2} x}{1 + \\tan^{2} x}dx &= \\int_{0}^{π/4}\\frac{1}{1 + \\tan^{2} x} \\cdot \\tan x\\sec^{2} xdx \\\\\n&= \\int_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(\\ln 2 - \\ln 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\ln 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533390 #edb/ref 17592186045417 false] ["Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator, because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(1+\\tan^2 x) = 2\\tan{x}\\sec^2 x\n$$\n\nSo, let\n\n$$\nu = 1+\\tan^2 x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2\\tan x\\sec^2 x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u = \\tan x\\sec^2 x\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi/4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{π/4}\\frac{\\tan x\\sec^{2} x}{1 + \\tan^{2} x}dx &= \\int_{0}^{π/4}\\frac{1}{1 + \\tan^{2} x} \\cdot \\tan x\\sec^{2} xdx \\\\\n&= \\int_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(\\ln 2 - \\ln 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\ln 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533349 #edb/ref 17592186045417 true] ["Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator, because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(1+\\tan^2 x) = 2\\tan{x}\\sec^2 x.\n$$\n\nSo, let $u = 1+\\tan^2 x.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x } = 2\\tan x\\sec^2 x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u = \\tan x\\sec^2 x\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi/4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x}{1+{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{1+{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533390 #edb/ref 17592186045417 true]}
